## :link: Tiny Little Office {.title}

The link is the document.

---

## What it is

- An office suite with no accounts and no server that holds your work
- Every document lives in the address of its page, after the #
- Copy the link and you have copied the document
- Open a link, change it, and the address bar is your own copy

> The browser never sends the part after the # to any server. That one fact is the whole design.

---

## How it is made {.two}

- Eleven apps on one kit
- One text format: Markdown with a few additions
- Any document can sit inside another: a chart on a slide, a map on a page

|

- Share: the link, a short link, a QR code
- Download: PDF, PNG, Markdown, PowerPoint, Word, Excel, OPML, GPX, ICS
- Open source under the MIT license

---

## :presentation: Presentation Builder {.icon}

- You are looking at it
- Ten looks: Boardroom, Ivory, Midnight, Graphite, Confetti, Sunset, Ocean, Bubblegum, Arcade, Chalkboard
- Prints as a zine, a booklet, a brochure, a comic, a poster or name badges
- Every deck ends with a QR code of itself

---

## :file-text: Word Processor

```write
# A letter to the block {.title}

Dear neighbors,

The stoop sale is **Saturday, October 24**, ten to four. Three jobs are still open:

- [x] Tables, from the church basement
- [ ] A sign for the corner^[Big letters. Last year nobody saw it.]
- [ ] Someone to mind the cash box

Yours, Rosa
```

> A page with a task list, a footnote and a table that adds up. It prints, and it downloads as Word.

---

## :list-tree: Outliner

```outline
- The link is the document
  - The problem {label="5 min"}
    - A file needs a place to live, an account to reach it, a server to keep it
    - Sharing a file is sharing a place
  - The idea {label="10 min"}
    - Put the whole document after the # in the address
    - Nothing is sent anywhere
    - Copy the link and you have copied the file
```

> Presented, an outline builds up an item at a time. The same text opens as a mind map or a deck.

